---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import RelatedLinks from "../../components/RelatedLinks.astro";
import ReferralCta from "../../components/ReferralCta.astro";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/+$/, "");

const related = [
  {
    href: `${base}/guides/where-to-place-starlink-dish/`,
    title: "Where to place your Starlink dish",
    description:
      "A practical walkthrough: direction, mount height, candidate spots, and what to check before you drill.",
  },
  {
    href: `${base}/guides/satellite-internet-for-remote-homes/`,
    title: "Satellite internet for remote homes",
    description:
      "How low-earth-orbit service works and why placement decides the experience at your address.",
  },
  {
    href: `${base}/planner/`,
    title: "The dish position map",
    description:
      "Drop a pin on satellite imagery and swing a 100° open-sky wedge clear of trees and rooflines.",
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Starlink Field of View and Obstructions, Explained"
  description="What Starlink's field of view requirement means: the roughly 100° open-sky window, elevation angles, why dishes point away from the equator, and how obstructions cause dropouts."
  path="/guides/starlink-field-of-view-obstructions/"
>
  <article class="article">
    <h1>Starlink's field of view and obstructions, explained</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Most placement advice stops at "keep it clear." This page explains what the dish actually
      needs to see — the open-sky window, how high off the horizon it must stay, and what happens
      when something stands in the way.
    </p>

    <h2>A moving constellation, not one satellite</h2>
    <p>
      A satellite TV dish aims at one satellite parked in a fixed spot. Starlink is the opposite:
      the dish serves a constellation of low-earth-orbit satellites that cross the sky continuously,
      handing your connection from one to the next. Instead of a pointing line, the dish needs a
      <strong>wide corridor of open sky</strong> — roughly <strong>100° across</strong> above the
      dish. Installation estimates for that field of view run from 90° to 110°, which is why the
      planner defaults to a 100° wedge and lets you rotate it.
    </p>

    <h2>Elevation: the 25° planning line</h2>
    <p>
      The window isn't only wide — it also has to be high enough off the horizon. Early Starlink
      guidance asked for a clear view starting about <strong>25° above the horizon</strong>, and
      that figure stuck as the rule of thumb. The constellation itself has since grown more
      forgiving: satellites in lower orbits reach your dish from lower angles, so today's placements
      tolerate more obstruction near the horizon than older forum guides claim.
    </p>
    <p>
      For planning, stay conservative: treat anything below about 25° as a bonus zone, not a
      requirement. A window that's clear from 25° upward isn't a marginal placement — it's one with
      margin.
    </p>

    <h2>Why the dish points away from the equator</h2>
    <p>
      Satellite density in the Starlink constellation rises toward its poleward limit — the orbits
      cluster as they approach their maximum inclination — and the dish's electronics are oriented
      accordingly. A dish in the northern hemisphere looks north; in the southern hemisphere, south.
      There's a second reason to keep the equatorial side low: geostationary satellites occupy a
      belt over the equator, and a Starlink dish pointing that way is aimed at sky the constellation
      barely uses.
    </p>
    <div class="callout">
      <h2>Where the "face south" advice comes from</h2>
      <p>
        Geostationary TV satellites sit over the equator, so TV dishes in the US face south.
        Starlink's dish points away from the equator for the reasons above — the official Starlink
        app shows the exact direction for your address and has the final word.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>What obstructions actually do</h2>
    <p>
      When a tree branch or roofline crosses the window, the connection doesn't just slow down — it
      drops. The dish waits out each blocked pass, and with satellites crossing every few seconds,
      even a thin obstruction in the wrong part of the sky turns into regular interruptions:
      streams freeze, calls drop, downloads stall. Trees are the worst offenders because foliage
      absorbs the signal; rooflines and outbuildings block outright; and seasonal leaf growth can
      turn a clear winter view into a summer problem.
    </p>

    <h2>Checking a view, in the right order</h2>
    <p>
      First scout on satellite imagery — the <a href={`${base}/planner/`}>dish position map</a>
      draws the 100° wedge over your property so you can judge sightlines from a desk or a phone,
      before any hardware exists. Then, once the dish is in hand, run the camera-based obstruction
      scan in the official Starlink app at the install spot. The app is the final word; the map
      planner gets you to it with a placement that's already likely to pass.
    </p>
  </article>

  <RelatedLinks links={related} />

  <ReferralCta
    heading="Check your own sky"
    body="Open the planner, drop a pin on your home, and see whether the 100° window fits between your trees."
  />
</BaseLayout>
